## 2026-08-09 - STEP 6 (CMS min-query + ε/δ sizing) + FIRST CHECKPOINT TAG
- what: query() = min of row counters (the tightest overestimate) + size_cms(ε,δ) → (width=⌈e/ε⌉, depth=⌈ln(1/δ)⌉). why: this completes the hero's core guarantee — now we can SIZE a sketch for a target accuracy AND QUERY it, and the additive error bound holds. concept: the ε·N additive error bound + min-estimator. next: step 7, memory accounting + serialize/deserialize.
- gate design note: the ε/δ guarantee is PER-KEY (for any fixed key x, P(error > ε·N) ≤ δ over hash randomness), not simultaneous across all keys. Initial test checked all keys per trial and saw 12.5% failure rate (union bound inflates it). Redesigned: 300 seeds, per-key failure rate checked on top-10 heavy hitters individually, each ≤ 3·δ margin. Passes cleanly.
- tag: green-sketchflow-1 cut at commit 1e6bae1 — 7 green steps (0–6), the first checkpoint per Rule 6.
- Commit 1e6bae1, CI 34s.

## 2026-08-10 - STEP 7 (CMS memory accounting + serialize/deserialize)
- what: bytes_used() measures real Python memory via recursive sys.getsizeof; to_bytes()/from_bytes() for compact binary round-trip. why: honest memory measurement matters — Python list-of-lists overhead is ~4× the packed minimum; comparing sketch vs exact-dict memory is meaningless without measuring what the interpreter actually allocated. concept: measuring real memory footprint honestly; serialization as the bridge between in-memory and on-disk. next: step 8, CMS inner-product / join-size estimate between two sketches.
- gate design note: bytes_used walks outer list + each inner list + every int cell with sys.getsizeof; test independently recomputes the same walk and asserts equality. Serialization uses struct.pack little-endian: 24-byte header (width u32 + depth u32 + seed i64 + total u64) then row-major uint64 counters. from_bytes validates header length and total blob size; corrupt inputs raise ValueError. 10 new tests, 38 total green.
- Commit 9912643, CI 35s.

## 2026-08-12 - STEP 8 (CMS inner-product / join-size estimate)
- what: inner_product(other) estimates the dot product of two frequency vectors via min of row-wise dot products. why: sketches are linear — two streams counted independently with the same hashes can be compared without seeing the raw data; this is the join-size estimator (how many matching pairs?). concept: linear sketches compose; sketches as vectors; join-size estimation. next: step 9, conservative-update CMS (increment only minimum cells to reduce overestimation).
- gate design note: error bound ε·‖a‖₁·‖b‖₁ tested across 200 hash seeds on two 5k Zipfian streams; empirical violation rate ≤ 3·δ margin. Also verified: never undercounts (est ≥ true), symmetric, self-join ≈ L2² (within 5% on low-collision setup), disjoint universes bounded, weighted adds correct, dimension/seed mismatches rejected. 11 new tests, 49 total green.
- reconciliation note: code was built by a prior run but left uncommitted in the working tree (orphaned build). Verified tests pass, committed and pushed as today's step.
- Commit 51397cc, CI 52s.

## 2026-08-13 - STEP 9 (conservative-update CMS)
- what: ConservativeUpdateCMS — subclass that overrides add() to only bump the lowest counters. why: standard CMS blindly increments all d counters, piling collision noise on already-inflated values; CU checks the current minimum estimate first and leaves higher counters alone, cutting overestimation without sacrificing the never-undercount guarantee. concept: conservative update reduces overestimation error (Estan & Varghese, SIGCOMM 2002). next: step 10, Space-Saving top-k (bounded-memory heavy hitters with monitored counters + eviction).
- gate design note: core gate tests CU ≤ plain CMS AND ≥ true count for every key on a 10k Zipfian stream (same hash seed, same stream). Also verified: total CU overestimation is strictly and meaningfully less than CMS (>20% reduction on skewed data); never-undercount holds across 50 seeds on a narrow sketch (width 64); weighted adds, serialization round-trip, inner_product, bytes_used all inherited correctly. 12 new tests, 61 total green.
- Commit 604496b, CI 54s.

## 2026-08-14 - STEP 10 (Space-Saving top-k)
- what: bounded-memory heavy hitter tracker — at most k monitored counters; evict the minimum when full and inherit its count. why: CMS estimates frequencies but doesn't tell you WHICH keys are heavy; Space-Saving answers "who are the top talkers?" with a hard memory cap. concept: the Space-Saving guarantee — every key with true count > N/k is in the monitored set (Metwally, Agrawal & El Abbadi, ICDT 2005). next: step 11, min-heap + hash index for O(log k) update (same API, faster internals).
- gate design note: core guarantee tested across 10 seeds on Zipfian streams — every key with true count > N/k present. Exact top-10 match with k=50 headroom (k=20 was too tight — boundary keys with close counts can swap due to eviction noise). Overestimate invariant verified: every monitored count ≥ true count. 19 new tests, 80 total green.
- Commit 1b8eb2b, CI 54s.

## 2026-08-15 - STEP 11 (Space-Saving min-heap + hash index)
- what: refactored SpaceSaving internals from dict+linear-scan to min-heap+hash index for O(log k) update. why: the step-10 linear min() scan is O(k) per eviction — fine for k=20, painful for k=2000+; the heap makes the data structure production-grade. concept: heap+hashmap combo for fast approximate top-k (the classic "indexed priority queue" pattern). next: step 12, CMS + Space-Saving combined engine (estimate counts AND surface heavy hitters end-to-end).
- implementation notes: heap entries are mutable [count, seq, key] lists; seq is a monotonic insertion counter for FIFO tie-breaking (matches step-10's dict-insertion-order min behavior). _index dict maps key→heap-entry for O(1) lookup. Bump = in-place count update + sift-down (count increased → entry may sink in min-heap). Eviction = overwrite root in-place + sift-down (avoids pop+push, one sift instead of two). Uses heapq._siftdown/_siftup for O(log k) partial sifts; falls back to heapify if private API unavailable.
- gate design note: (1) heap invariant verified after every mutation pattern: sequential inserts, repeated bumps, 200-step eviction cycles, Zipfian workloads, weighted adds, k=1 edge case. (2) All step-10 behavioral tests re-run and pass identically. (3) Throughput scaling: at k=2000, throughput stays >15% of k=50 (measured ratio ~0.5, consistent with O(log k); O(k) linear scan would give ~0.025). 16 new tests, 96 total green.
- Commit 139b17b, CI 53s.

## 2026-08-17 - STEP 12 (CMS + Space-Saving combined engine)
- what: SketchEngine class that composes CMS (or CU-CMS) + SpaceSaving into a single-pass streaming analytics engine. why: individual structures answer different questions — CMS knows "how many of X?" while Space-Saving knows "who's on top?" — composing them gives a complete traffic-analysis pipeline from one ingestion pass. concept: composing sketches into an analytics engine; the standard "frequency sketch + top-k tracker" pattern used in production stream-processing systems. next: step 13, benchmark harness v1 (Phase P4 — exact baseline vs CMS/CU/SS over synthetic streams, emit CSV).
- design notes: auto-sizes CMS from (ε,δ) via size_cms() so the caller specifies accuracy guarantees, not raw dimensions. heavy_hitters() returns Space-Saving's top-k keys but re-estimates their counts via CMS, which is tighter (especially with CU). Supports conservative=True to swap in ConservativeUpdateCMS. The .total property reads from CMS (single source of truth); tests verify all three totals (engine, CMS, SS) stay in sync.
- gate design note: core gate tests engine top-10 matches exact top-10 across 10 random seeds on 10k Zipfian streams (with k=50 headroom). Also verified: heavy_hitters counts come from CMS (not SS); all CMS estimates are overestimates (never-undercount); CU mode strictly reduces overestimation vs plain; weighted adds equivalent to repeated singles; edge cases (empty stream, single item, k=1); summary dict well-formed; estimate() == cms.query(); bad parameters raise ValueError. 18 new tests, 114 total green. Phase P3 (Conservative-update + Space-Saving top-k) now complete.
- Commit 099e86f, CI 50s.

## 2026-08-18 - STEP 13 (benchmark harness v1)
- what: bench.py — reproducible benchmark harness that runs seeded Zipfian streams through four sketch variants (CMS, CU-CMS, Engine, Engine-CU) and measures per-key error + memory against ExactCounter ground truth. why: until now each structure was tested for correctness in isolation; the harness compares them HEAD-TO-HEAD on the same stream, producing the accuracy-vs-memory CSV that the empirical study will build on. concept: reproducible experiment design — seeded streams, tidy CSV output, no GUI or prompts, one command regenerates all results. next: step 14, accuracy-vs-memory sweep (vary width/depth, plot error decay curve via matplotlib).
- key findings from first run (report/results.csv, 50k Zipfian stream, α=1.2): CU-CMS cuts mean absolute error roughly in half vs plain CMS across all ε values (e.g. 12.56 vs 24.46 at ε=0.01). At ε=0.001 the CMS uses ~1.39× the memory of the exact dict — an honest reflection of Python list-of-lists overhead vs a compact hash table. Raw CMS finds exact top-10 by querying all keys; Engine with k=10 SpaceSaving gets 6/10 (SpaceSaving guarantee threshold N/k=5000 is too high for some borderline heavy hitters).
- gate design note: 14 new tests verify: headless run produces BenchRow objects; write_csv creates valid CSV with error+memory columns; errors non-negative; memory positive; CU ≤ CMS error; reproducibility (same seed → identical output); CLI entry point; multi-epsilon sweep; smaller ε uses more memory. 128 total tests green.
- Commit 0bd3355, CI 44s.
